FAQ = [
 ("What is the best roof rack for a Tesla Model 3?",
  "Tesla's own Model 3 Roof Rack sets the benchmark: $400 on the Tesla Shop, a 150 lb load rating, a 495 lb static limit, T-slot aluminum bars and die-cast towers with integrated locks, and Tesla says it fits all Model 3 vehicles. It isn't sold on Amazon under a Model 3 listing we could confirm, so the Amazon picks here are aftermarket. Of those, the WheelX lockable set is the first choice because its listing covers 2017–2026, Highland included."),
 ("Does a 2017–2023 Model 3 roof rack fit the 2024 Highland?",
  "Don't assume it does. Makers that publish fitment split the car in two. Tesstudio sells separate versions for the 2017–2023.10 Model 3 and the Highland from 2023.11 builds, and TESEVO lists Model 3 2017–2023.10 and Highland 2024+ separately. Tesla says its own rack fits all Model 3. For aftermarket bars, buy a listing that names 2024 or later, or ask the seller which fit kit ships for your build date."),
 ("How much weight can a Model 3 roof carry?",
  "Tesla rates its Model 3 rack at 150 lb while driving and 495 lb static (parked). The dynamic figure is the one that matters for bikes, skis and boxes, and it includes the bars and carriers. Some aftermarket bars list 165–220 lb, but those are ratings for the bars, not the car. Keep the total on the roof at or under Tesla's 150 lb whatever the bar says."),
 ("Can I put a rooftop tent on a Model 3?",
  "Tesla's 495 lb static rating is for a parked car, which is the situation a tent is in, so the roof itself isn't the obstacle. Two things are. The tent must weigh 150 lb or less to be legal on the road under the dynamic rating, and your bars must carry a static rating of their own. Ask the rack maker for a written static figure before you buy a tent."),
 ("How much range does a roof rack cost on a Model 3?",
  "Tesla publishes no figure for its rack. The best public data is for gas cars: fueleconomy.gov says rooftop cargo boxes cut efficiency by 2–8% in the city, 6–17% on the highway and 10–25% at 65–75 mph, while rear-mounted carriers cost 1–5% on the highway. The Model 3 is a low, slippery sedan, so anything on its roof adds a larger share of total drag than on an SUV."),
 ("What crossbar spread does the Model 3 have?",
  "About 28 in. etrailer's product experts cite a 28 in crossbar spacing on the Model 3 when fitting a Yakima SkyBox 21 cargo box to factory and aftermarket racks. That is short for a long box. Check the spread range your box lists, and choose a shorter box if it would otherwise overhang the windshield or hit the trunk lid when it opens."),
 ("Will a Thule or Yakima rack fit a Model 3?",
  "Yakima sells a complete BaseLine system for the Model 3 through etrailer: JetStream bars, BaseLine towers and the Y06173 BaseClip kit, listed at $694.80 with a 165 lb rating. It clips to the door jambs rather than using the fixed points. We found no Amazon listing that names the Model 3 for it, so it isn't a pick here. Yakima carriers also fit Tesla's bars, but etrailer says the aero factory bars need 23H adapters."),
 ("Do I need to drill or will it damage the glass?",
  "No drilling. Fixed-point racks bolt into metal receivers under the roof trim, and the towers rest on the body at those points, not on the glass. Door-jamb systems such as Yakima's BaseLine use padded clips and rubber pads. Either way, clean the roof and the pads before you install, because grit under a pad is what scratches paint."),
 ("Can I use a hitch bike rack instead?",
  "Yes, and it costs less range. Our vehicle data notes the US Model 3 has no factory hitch, but aftermarket receivers are sold for bike racks. fueleconomy.gov puts rear-mounted carriers at 1–5% worse on the highway, against 6–17% for a rooftop box. A roof rack still makes sense for skis, kayaks or a narrow cargo box."),
 ("Is the Model 3 roof rack noisy?",
  "It can be. The Model 3 cabin is quiet, so a bar that whistles is easy to hear. Tesla says its rack was designed for minimal noise, and the aftermarket listings here sell aero profiles for the same reason, but none of them publishes a decibel figure. Keep the rubber strips fully seated in the T-slot, fit the end caps, and take the bars off when you don't need them."),
]

